\chapter{Security: random numbers, the time of day and TLS (HTTPS)}
\label{ch:tls}

A telnet session sends the password in clear text. A program that fetches a web page over the Internet has to know that the
page comes from the server it asked, and that nobody on the way reads or changes it. \emph{TLS} (Transport Layer Security, RFC 5246
for version 1.2) does that on top of a TCP connection: the two sides agree on keys, the server proves who it is with a
\emph{certificate}, and everything after that is encrypted and protected against changes. \emph{HTTPS} is the web protocol HTTP spoken
through TLS.

TLS needs four things that a small system like Ulix did not have yet, and each of them is a section of this chapter:

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Random numbers that nobody can guess} (the keys are made from them), from a hardware generator ([[random.c]]).
\item \textbf{The time of day.} A certificate is valid from one date to another, and the Raspberry Pi has no battery clock, so
      after the boot it does not know the date. [[sntp.c]] asks a time server.
\item \textbf{TCP connections for programs.} The kernel has TCP since the chapter on networking, but only for its own services
      (telnet, echo). A few system calls give programs a socket ([[net_syscalls.c]]).
\item \textbf{The TLS code itself.} It is not written for this book. Writing a cryptographic library that is correct, and does not
      leak secrets through timing, is a job for specialists. We use \emph{BearSSL} (Thomas Pornin, MIT licence), which is
      small, written to be read, needs no [[malloc]] and only five functions of the C library. It is an unmodified git
      submodule in [[third_party/bearssl]]; what is ours is the glue (a few small files) and the program [[https]].
\end{enumerate}

\paragraph{What is supported, and what is not.} The client speaks \textbf{TLS 1.2} (BearSSL has no TLS 1.3 yet). TLS 1.0 and 1.1 are
switched off in the client (they are deprecated since RFC 8996), and \textbf{SSL 2.0 and SSL 3.0 are not implemented, on purpose}: both
are broken (for example the POODLE attack on SSL 3.0), BearSSL does not contain them, and no web server should accept them today.
The certificate chain is checked against the certificate authorities in [[/etc/ssl/cacert.pem]], the validity dates against the
clock, and the host name against the certificate.

\paragraph{The switch.} TLS can be switched off in two places, in the same way as the telnet server. At run time,
[[/etc/rc.conf]] has the line [[tls_enable="YES"]] (this is also the default when the file or the line is missing); with
[[tls_enable="NO"]] the program [[https]] says ``TLS is disabled'' and stops. At build time, [[make TLS=0]] leaves the library and the
glue out completely: [[https]] is then a tiny program that only says that this system was built without TLS, and BearSSL is
not even compiled.

@
\section{Random numbers (see \texttt{random.c})}
\label{src:random.c}

Keys must not be guessable. A program that computes ``random'' numbers from the clock gives an attacker a few thousand possibilities
to try. The numbers have to come from a physical process, and that is what a \emph{hardware random number generator} measures (noise
in a circuit). Two are supported:

\begin{itemize}
\item the generator of the Raspberry Pi 4 (``RNG200'', in the device tree as [[brcm,bcm2711-rng200]]). The kernel starts it and
      reads 32 bit words from its FIFO. \textbf{This driver is written from the register description and has not run on a board yet}
      (qemu does not have this device);
\item the \texttt{virtio-rng} device of qemu's ``virt'' machine (device number 4): the driver gives the device a buffer and the device
      fills it with bytes from the random number generator of the host. This is how the tests get their numbers. Start qemu with
      [[-device virtio-rng-device]].
\end{itemize}

If the machine has neither, [[random_get]] returns $-1$. We do not make up numbers in that case: the programs that need them (the
TLS client) stop with a message, instead of working with keys that look secure and are not. For the same reason there is no
``mixing pool'' and no clock-based fallback here. The kernel hands out what the hardware gave, and the program (BearSSL) feeds it
into its own pseudo random generator (HMAC\_DRBG) as a seed.

The virtio part is the same as in [[virtionet.c]]: one queue, but with a single buffer that the \emph{device} writes
(flag [[VIRTQ_DESC_F_WRITE]]).

@

The complete file is put together from these parts:

